1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:04,360
You Are a Meat Robot The usual objection to curing all disease is that it's impossible.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:10,720
You replace three hundred and thirty billion cells a day. You three-D-print windpipes.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:17,900
You reprogram skin into brain cells. You've already proven your body is a machine, and machines are repairable.

4
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:22,660
The only reason you haven't repaired it is that you spent the repair budget on missiles.

5
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:30,340
This chapter removes the "impossible" objection so you can get back to the "expensive" objection, which the treaty solves.

6
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:36,460
Your body is a machine. Not "like" a machine. A machine. Blueprint: D.N.A.

7
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:42,040
Moving parts: organs. Fuel: whatever you ate for lunch (usually something beige).

8
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:49,960
Owner's manual: your genome, which you only discovered twenty years ago, in a drawer you hadn't opened for two hundred thousand

9
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:55,280
years. Before that you were just shaking it and hoping. This is called "medicine.".

10
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:04,319
Your D.N.A. is a program written three point eight billion years ago by a process with no quality assurance team, no project

11
00:01:04,319 --> 00:01:08,270
manager, and no version control. It has never been debugged.

12
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:15,420
It has never been patched. It contains code from organisms that went extinct five hundred million years ago.

13
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:22,480
You are running legacy software that predates multicellular life, and you are surprised when it crashes.

14
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:27,800
That's like being surprised when a three point eight billion year old car breaks down.

15
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:33,790
The surprising part is that it starts. Death is a Technical Problem.

16
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:40,980
Every religion and philosopher has sold you the same story: death is natural, inevitable, and meaningful.

17
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:48,240
Very comforting. Also wrong. Death is what happens when your parts break faster than your body can fix them.

18
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:54,480
That's not philosophy. That's a maintenance schedule. Every disease is a mechanical failure.

19
00:01:54,720 --> 00:02:02,560
Every ailment is a broken part. Every slow decline into decrepitude is an engineering problem with an engineering solution.

20
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:10,400
You just keep filing them under "philosophy" and "the human condition," which is like filing a flat tire under "existentialism.".

21
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:16,080
You've spent two hundred thousand years writing poetry about death.

22
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:20,760
You could have spent it building a wrench. You did eventually build the wrench.

23
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:26,150
Then you wrote poetry about the wrench. You Are a Self-Repairing Meat Robot.

24
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:31,620
Your D.N.A. is three billion letters of code. Not "like" code.

25
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:38,660
It IS code. You learned to read it (the Human Genome Project) and you're learning to edit it (CRISPR).

26
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:43,340
You are mechanics who finally found the service manual under the seat.

27
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:51,000
The service manual is written in a language you only partially understand, in a font that's three point eight billion years

28
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:59,460
old. But still. Progress. Every day, your body runs a maintenance routine that would make a German car engineer weep with

29
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:07,440
joy and then immediately resign out of inadequacy: You replace three hundred and thirty billion cells daily.

30
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:15,260
That's three hundred and thirty billion. With a B. Every day you throw away more cells than there are stars in the Milky

31
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:20,800
Way and grow new ones before lunch. You repair ten thousand D.N.A.

32
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:26,540
damage events in every cell, every day. You have thirty-seven trillion cells.

33
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:34,640
That's three hundred and seventy quadrillion repairs per day, performed by molecules that have never been to medical school.

34
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:42,650
You rebuild your entire skeleton every ten years. You are not the same skeleton you were a decade ago.

35
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:46,520
You are a ship of Theseus made of calcium and poor decisions.

36
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:53,520
You are not a static object. You are a pattern that persists while matter flows through you.

37
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,720
Like a river, except the river is meat and it's arguing with its insurance company.

38
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:03,500
Aging is what happens when the river starts cutting corners on its own maintenance.

39
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:10,760
You are literally dissolving at the molecular level, right now, while reading this sentence.

40
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:17,100
It's fine. (It is not fine.). You've Already Started Fixing the Machine.

41
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:26,060
Exhibit A: You Can Grow New Parts. A kid in Michigan has a three-D-printed windpipe keeping him alive.

42
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:35,170
You print custom titanium skulls and jaws. You grow bladders and blood vessels in labs and install them in humans.

43
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:44,300
Like replacement parts. For people. From a printer. The same technology you use to print novelty phone cases, you're using

44
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:52,300
to print throats. You are manufacturing replacement human organs on the same type of machine that makes Yoda figurines.

45
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:57,290
If that doesn't prove you're a machine, I'm not sure what level of evidence you require.

46
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:01,200
Perhaps someone could print you a new brain so you can process it.

47
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:11,000
Exhibit B: You Can Reprogram Your Cells. In two thousand and six, Shinya Yamanaka figured out how to turn adult cells back

48
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,140
into stem cells. Any cell. From anywhere in your body. Back to factory settings.

49
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:21,930
Like holding down the reset button, except on a skin cell. And it works.

50
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:29,100
This means: Your skin cell can become a heart cell. Your blood cell can become a brain cell.

51
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:37,420
Any cell can become any other cell. It's like discovering every part of your car can transform into any other part.

52
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:42,100
Need a new transmission? Just reprogram your floor mat. Need a new engine?

53
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:46,460
Your cup holder has been an engine this entire time. It just forgot.

54
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:54,960
You won a Nobel Prize for proving that your cells have no idea what they are and can be talked into being something else.

55
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:59,100
This is the most important discovery in the history of medicine.

56
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:08,420
You celebrated by going back to mostly not using it. Exhibit C: You're Debugging Your Code.

57
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:17,260
Gene therapy is literally finding typos in your D.N.A. and correcting them: Luxturna (twenty seventeen): Fixes blindness

58
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:22,720
caused by a mutated retinal pigment epithelium sixty-five, or R.P.E.

59
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:30,150
sixty-five gene. One injection. Sight restored. The entire disease was a typo.

60
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:34,360
One letter wrong, and you couldn't see. They fixed the letter.

61
00:06:35,260 --> 00:06:40,560
Zolgensma (twenty nineteen): Fixes spinal muscular atrophy.

62
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:48,560
Babies who would die before age two now walk. The disease was a spelling error in one gene.

63
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:52,550
You corrected the spelling. The baby walks. That's the whole story.

64
00:06:53,600 --> 00:07:02,840
Chimeric antigen receptor T-cell therapy, or CAR-T therapy (twenty seventeen): Reprograms your immune system to hunt cancer.

65
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:09,380
Complete remission in "incurable" cases. You taught your white blood cells to be assassins.

66
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:15,040
They were already assassins, technically, but now they're assassins with a target.

67
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:20,200
You're not treating symptoms anymore. You're fixing the actual code.

68
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:27,120
That's like going from "the car makes a weird noise, turn up the radio" to "the car makes a weird noise, let me look at

69
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:31,780
the engine." It only took you two hundred thousand years to try the second approach.

70
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:41,020
The Car Restoration Analogy. Think of your body as a nineteen eighty-seven Honda Civic with three point eight billion miles

71
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:46,360
on it. You already know how to fix most of the parts: Joints: Like worn brake pads.

72
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:51,780
You pop them out and put new ones in. You've been doing this since the nineteen sixties.

73
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:57,660
It's boring now. Boring is good. Boring means you solved it.

74
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:05,060
Arteries: Like clogged fuel lines. You shove a tiny metal tube (a "stent") in there and call it fixed.

75
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:08,660
This works surprisingly well for something that sounds like plumbing.

76
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:17,480
Hearts: Like a dead battery. You take one from another car (a car that no longer needs it, for reasons) and put it in yours.

77
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:22,440
This is called a "transplant," which is a polite word for organ recycling.

78
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:31,000
You're making progress on the harder stuff, too. Cancer is frame damage, and immunotherapy is your new welding torch.

79
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:36,440
Alzheimer's is a bad transmission, and you're finally starting to understand the gears.

80
00:08:37,340 --> 00:08:43,059
The point is: nobody looks at a broken car and says, "Well, that's just the car's journey.

81
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:48,240
The car lived a good life. We should accept the car's natural transition into rust."

82
00:08:48,920 --> 00:08:54,880
You call a mechanic. You are a car. Call a mechanic. Fund the mechanic.

83
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:03,520
The mechanic is medical research and you are paying the mechanic almost nothing while spending your money on missiles, which

84
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:08,830
are not a car repair tool even in the loosest interpretation of the word "repair.".

85
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:14,520
The Proof That Aging is Reversible. Nature Already Does It.

86
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:20,100
Planarian worms: Cut one in half, get two worms. Both younger than the original.

87
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:23,520
It's like cutting your car in half and getting two newer cars.

88
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:29,360
This is obviously impossible, except it happens every day in labs worldwide.

89
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:35,260
Naked mole rats: Live ten times longer than similar-sized mammals.

90
00:09:35,780 --> 00:09:42,260
Don't get cancer. They look like a thumb that's been left in the bath too long, but they've solved cancer.

91
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:50,900
You have not. Bowhead whales: Live two hundred plus years with no signs of age-related disease.

92
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:58,980
A whale born when Napoleon was alive is still swimming around right now, perfectly healthy, probably judging you.

93
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:08,740
Hydra: Biologically immortal. A tiny freshwater polyp with no brain has cracked the code to eternal life.

94
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:14,220
You have a brain. You have not cracked the code. Draw your own conclusions.

95
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:21,880
Nature solved aging. Multiple times. In multiple species. Using multiple strategies.

96
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:26,960
You just need to steal the answer, which should be easy because you've stolen everything else.

97
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:33,740
You've had empires. You stole entire continents. Surely you can steal a jellyfish's homework.

98
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:39,400
You've Already Reversed Aging (In Mice... and Human Cells).

99
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:46,260
You've done it: Significant lifespan extension in aged mice using Yamanaka factors.

100
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:57,480
thirty-year epigenetic age reversal in human skin cells. Vision restored in blind mice; not slowed, reversed You made old

101
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:06,420
mice young again. You made old human cells young again. You made blind mice see again (which, if you think about it, means

102
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:11,230
the nursery rhyme is now a research paper). The farmer's wife is going to be furious.

103
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,480
The mechanisms are understood. The techniques exist. You've demonstrated them in living organisms.

104
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:26,060
This is not theoretical. This is not science fiction. This is science fact that you proved, published, won prizes for, and

105
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:30,360
then filed under "interesting" before going back to building submarines.

106
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:35,310
You're not waiting for a breakthrough. You're waiting for funding.

107
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:41,680
Which is like having the cure for death in your pocket and not using it because you can't find exact change.
